{
  "character": "信",
  "summary": {
    "text": "信 means “trust” or “believe” and combines a person, 人, with words, 言. The familiar explanation is that one trusts a person’s words. That is an old interpretation, but another account gives 人 a sound-bearing role. The components are identifiable; their original jobs are disputed.",
    "evidence_ids": [
      "E1",
      "E2",
      "E3",
      "E6"
    ]
  },
  "history": [
    {
      "text": "The Shuowen defines 信 through sincerity and treats the two components as jointly conveying meaning. Wiktionary instead describes 言 as the meaning component and 人 as the phonetic component, connecting the latter with an older pronunciation. These accounts offer different explanations for the same construction.",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "E2",
        "E3"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    {
      "text": "The evidence here does not settle whether 人 was chosen for meaning, sound, or some combination. “A person’s words can be trusted” is useful for remembering 信, but should be presented as the traditional interpretation rather than an established reconstruction of its invention.",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "E1",
        "E2",
        "E3"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
